  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nUpper low will be nearly stationary along the New Jersey coast\nthe next 24 hours before it lifts northeast across New England\non Monday. Guidance has been consistent on pushing wrap-around\nclouds and precipitation in warm advection regime into mainly\nthe northeast portion of the forecast area by this evening.\nLight rain is likely in the Jefferson and Clarion County areas,\nwith a slight chance elsewhere along the ridges and generally\neast of Pittsburgh.  \n\nLow level dry air has been in place below 500 mb and that will\nlimit the western extent of measurable precipitation tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCome tuesday night, clouds associated with the nor'easter will \nbe out of our area. This allows a transitory H5 ridge axis to \nmove in returning dry and warmer weather. Thursday will be the \nwarmest day of the week with widespread 70s. Latest probs of \nmaxT 80F or greater continue to increase for northern WV, \nsoutheastern OH, and southwestern PA as they now reside between \n40-60%. This by no means is near record territory as benchmarks \nfor 1 October are still near 90F at most climate sites. Heat \nrisk at this juncture remains in the minor category. \n\nWe reside just south of a baroclinic zone which is along Lake Erie \ninto upstate New York placing us in the warm sector Friday and \nSaturday. The trend continues for dry weather, however a weak \nimpulse could traverse generating scattered showers.  For now, probs \nbetween 20-40% have been consistent in the warm sector.  With \nincreased moisture in the column, forecast highs pull back a little \non day 6 and 7 to the lower to mid 70s, while overnight lows near 60 \nare projected with above average confidence. \n\nFor locations who do not receive rainfall Sunday night or Monday \nthen none is expected the balance of September.",
